    "lawCode": "Amd §§1.20 & 150.50, CP L",
    "lawSection": "Criminal Procedure Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5078\n\nSPONSOR: KAPLAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the criminal procedure law, in\nrelation to the definition of an accusatory instrument\n\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to give parking tickets the necessary valid-\nity in law as an accusatory instrument in criminal court.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Subdivision 1 of section 1.20 of the criminal procedure law,\nas amended by chapter 209 of the laws of 1990, is amended to add para-\ngraph (b) to define an appearance ticket issued for a traffic infraction\nrelated to parking to the definition of an accusatory instrument, and to\nlist the required elements for an appearance ticket to qualify as an\naccusatory instrument.\n\n§ 2. Subdivision 1 of section 150.50 of the criminal procedure law, as\namended by chapter 549 of the laws of 1987, is amended to clarify that\nno separate accusatory instrument shall be required to be filed for an\nappearance ticket issued for a traffic infraction which conforms to the\nrequirements set forth in paragraph (b) of sub-division one of section\n1.20 of the chapter.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION (IF APPLICABLE):\n\nThe amendment creates paragraph (b) of Subdivision I of section 1.20 of\nthe criminal procedure law, as amended by chapter 209 of the laws of\n1990, to define an appearance ticket issued for a traffic infraction\nrelated to parking to the definition of an accusatory instrument, and to\nlist the required elements for an appearance ticket to qualify as an\naccusatory instrument. The amendment also clarifies that no separate\naccusatory instrument shall be required to be filed for an appearance\nticket issued for a traffic infraction which conforms to the require-\nments set forth in paragraph (b) of subdivision one of section 1.20 of\nthe chapter.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nRecent appellate court decisions, People v. Carrillo, 55 Misc 3d 147(A),\n2017 NY Slip Op 50703(U)(App Term, 2\"d Dept, 9th & 10th Jud Dists 2017)\nand People v. Tarnoff 54 Misc 3d139(A), 2017 Slip Op 50151(U) (App Term,\n2nd dept, 9th & 10th Jud Dists 2017) invalidated parking tickets for\nlack of jurisdiction over the defendant when the courts held that the\nticket was the functional equivalent of an appearance ticket and there-\nfore insufficient under criminal law as an accusatory instrument. Anoth-\ner recent decision, People v. Mann 55 Misc 3d 134(A), 2017 Slip Op\n50484(U) found that the parking ticket was sufficient as an accusatory\ninstrument but reversed the conviction based on improper evidence at\ntrial. As this conflict in analysis can be found in additional cases\ngoing back twenty years, there is clearly a need to clarify this issue\nin statute. This bill will ensure that an appearance ticket issued for a\nparking infraction is legally valid as an accusatory instrument and that\nno further accusatory instrument is required. Municipalities must also\nhave the ability to enforce parking rules and regulations in order to\nensure that there is sufficient available parking for residents and\nbusinesses.  Additionally, there must be a flow of available parking for\nsmall local business to succeed. This bill will give parking tickets the\nnecessary validity under law to enforce parking regulations without\nrequiring additional and unnecessary court filings. Parking tickets are\nalso a vital source of revenue for municipalities and this bill will\nprotect their ability to collect those funds.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017-18: A8687-A (Otis)/S9075 (LAVALLE), passed assembly/REFERRED TO\nCODES\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nThis bill will ensure that local governments are able to enforce parking\nregulations which are an important source of revenue. The fiscal impact\nwill vary by municipality.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
